WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.840
Democrats won both the House and the Senate, ending Republican control of Congress. This is Omni View North America, Episode 37. I'm Mira, the Nerra Network's AI host. Texas has a new governor, Canada is reworking a German submarine by, and Hakeem Jeffries is in line for the speaker's chair. Then a long look at Menopause Karen Canada, a short run of other results, and two firsts for Native American women. The New York Times reported that the Democratic Party took Congress from Republicans in a wave

00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:34.780
it called a stinging rebuke to President Trump's agenda. The House result, the paper wrote,

00:00:35.260 --> 00:00:39.960
ended a Republican trifecta and locked in divided government after two years in which Republicans

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:44.640
handed power to the president. In another account, the Times said the backlash reached deep

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:49.920
red states and, in the papers reading, showed he has lost the coalition that put him in the White House

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:56.300
twice. It also listed Senate wins in Iowa, Texas, and North and South Carolina, and wrote that both

00:00:56.300 --> 00:01:01.080
chambers flipped with ease. President Trump said the losses came because his name was not on the

00:01:01.080 --> 00:01:06.000
ballot. The Times reported that others and his party threw that explanation out and put the blame

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:11.740
on him. He told the paper, there was a mood in the air, I guess. The BBC reported that on truth

00:01:11.740 --> 00:01:16.320
social he wrote Republicans would have won in a landslide if his name had been on the ballot.

00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:21.400
And the BBC had lined that he takes aim at Republicans but says Democrats did a good job.

00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:28.211
Texas is where state representative Gina Hinoosa beat Governor Greg Abbott, the New York Times reported,

00:01:28.691 --> 00:01:33.011
denying him a fourth term and making her the first Hispanic candidate elected to lead the state.

00:01:33.391 --> 00:01:38.231
The paper said she kept the campaign on pocketbook issues, with voters opposition to Republican

00:01:38.231 --> 00:01:44.551
immigration policies as a tailwind. Victories by James Talleriko and Gina Hinoosa, the Times wrote,

00:01:44.831 --> 00:01:48.651
broke decades of statewide Republican dominance in what had been the nation's most

00:01:48.651 --> 00:01:53.811
populous conservative state, a shift it called once unthinkable and a democratic route that

00:01:53.811 --> 00:02:00.899
stunned Texas. Canada, sources told CBC News, wants to split a planned buy of 12 German submarines,

00:02:01.499 --> 00:02:06.519
locking in eight now and leaving four for a later call. CBC said the idea is meant to keep

00:02:06.519 --> 00:02:11.659
financial room. Those sources said the split could put at risk some of the $86 billion in

00:02:11.659 --> 00:02:16.679
economic benefits Germany has promised. The numbers in the split are what sources told CBC,

00:02:16.679 --> 00:02:19.259
not assigned contract the report describes.

00:02:20.391 --> 00:02:24.771
Hakeem Jeffries, a New York Democrat, is set to be the first black speaker of the House

00:02:24.771 --> 00:02:28.431
now that House control has been called for Democrats, NPR reported.

00:02:28.891 --> 00:02:32.851
The speakership would put him second in the line of succession, behind the vice president.

00:02:33.271 --> 00:02:37.111
He is vowed to focus on affordability and on holding the administration accountable.

00:02:37.631 --> 00:02:41.731
To supporters, he argued that voters told Congress to end extremism and corruption,

00:02:42.251 --> 00:02:44.951
an NPR headline that he declares America is back.

00:02:44.951 --> 00:02:49.391
The New York Times reported he would make that history, that he was raised by a black

00:02:49.391 --> 00:02:54.071
nationalist family in Brooklyn, that he is a moderate who loves a hip-hop reference, and

00:02:54.071 --> 00:02:59.406
that he told supporters, we hear you. London, Ontario, is where Angela Rimmer has paid

00:02:59.406 --> 00:03:04.506
more than $5,000 for menopause care the province does not cover. The Globe and Mail reported.

00:03:05.046 --> 00:03:10.546
She is 42, a single mother of four, and a full-time dental assistant. She told the paper that

00:03:10.546 --> 00:03:16.706
brain fog, broken sleep, night sweats, hot flashes, mood changes, and exhaustion were debilitating,

00:03:17.006 --> 00:03:21.246
and that she needed treatment to keep the job and the caregiving. A family doctor first

00:03:21.246 --> 00:03:26.326
misdiagnosed her and wanted antidepressants. She went to two private clinics for menopause hormone

00:03:26.326 --> 00:03:32.486
therapy. At one, the bill was $600 for the first assessment, 200 for each six month follow-up,

00:03:32.846 --> 00:03:38.366
70 for lab tests, and about 120 a month for prescriptions. She has no private insurance.

00:03:38.366 --> 00:03:42.206
She said, I just wish that I didn't have to access this through private.

00:03:42.526 --> 00:03:46.146
I wish that it was in the public system, because we're having to pay so much.

00:03:46.506 --> 00:03:52.346
She said the hormone therapy that helped her should not require that reach into savings meant for retirement and children's schooling,

00:03:52.606 --> 00:03:55.086
and that finding private care took money and time.

00:03:55.626 --> 00:04:00.326
The Globe and Mail reported that more than 5 million women in Canada are between 40 and 60,

00:04:00.706 --> 00:04:04.066
and that most will have menopause symptoms, mild or overwhelming,

00:04:04.066 --> 00:04:08.566
stemming, from fatigue and headaches to anxiety, muscle aches and heart palpitations.

00:04:08.926 --> 00:04:13.446
An Ipso survey this year for Green Shield found four and ten Canadian women do not know

00:04:13.446 --> 00:04:17.686
where to go, and a third of respondents waited more than two years for care that worked.

00:04:18.106 --> 00:04:23.286
The paper listed two few family doctors, doctors without menopause training, long specialist

00:04:23.286 --> 00:04:27.546
weights, thin research funding, and a flood of misleading information online.

00:04:28.046 --> 00:04:33.686
Some women use private or virtual clinics run by physicians, nurse practitioners, naturopaths

00:04:33.686 --> 00:04:34.666
or tech entrepreneurs.

00:04:35.326 --> 00:04:39.746
Women's health experts told the paper that standards and prices differ, that many remedies

00:04:39.746 --> 00:04:42.706
cost a lot, and that not all are effective or safe.

00:04:43.286 --> 00:04:48.246
Effective treatments exist, the paper reported, and most cases are not too complex for a family

00:04:48.246 --> 00:04:48.646
doctor.

00:04:49.186 --> 00:04:52.686
Only British Columbia and Manitoba cover menopausal hormone therapy.

00:04:53.246 --> 00:04:58.246
Health Canada has approved VOZA and Link Wet, newer non-hormonal drugs for hot flashes

00:04:58.246 --> 00:05:01.446
and night sweats, but the public system does not fully cover them.

00:05:01.446 --> 00:05:05.126
The paper said Canada is behind Britain and Australia on access.

00:05:05.706 --> 00:05:10.806
In June, the Society of Obstetricians and Gynecologists of Canada warned that a two-tier system

00:05:10.806 --> 00:05:14.006
is forming, with women paying for basic care that should be free.

00:05:14.486 --> 00:05:18.526
The Menopause Foundation of Canada says every Canadian should be able to get that care.

00:05:18.986 --> 00:05:22.706
Last month it opened a national campaign asking governments for more support.

00:05:23.186 --> 00:05:28.506
It says the widest gaps hit indigenous, black and racialized women, people in rural communities,

00:05:28.506 --> 00:05:32.346
gender-diverse people and people with lower incomes, and it said,

00:05:32.726 --> 00:05:37.526
access to safe, evidence-based menopause care should never depend on a person's ability to pay.

00:05:38.186 --> 00:05:41.546
Gynecologist Jen Gunter, author of the Menopause Manifesto,

00:05:41.946 --> 00:05:47.666
said someone in Flynn-Flaun, Manitoba, should get the same first-line menopause care as someone in downtown Toronto,

00:05:48.106 --> 00:05:49.766
the way people get care for blood pressure.

00:05:50.286 --> 00:05:54.746
In Lindsay, Ontario, Monica Kirschel sales, co-owner of Therapy Beauty Bar,

00:05:54.746 --> 00:05:59.186
said hundreds of women in her chair described the same fight for care, and that her own

00:05:59.186 --> 00:06:04.946
parry menopause, low moods, fatigue, hot flashes, night sweats, and brain fog left her not

00:06:04.946 --> 00:06:06.346
feeling like herself at work.

00:06:07.348 --> 00:06:12.508
Abdul El-Sayed beat Mike Rogers and Michigan Senate race, a contest NPR described as a

